# Project Rules

## Core Principles

- Optimize for files that are easy to open, scan, and change.
- Keep code close to the route or feature that owns it. Promote code only after real reuse.
- Prefer small, cohesive modules over large mixed-purpose files, but avoid one-line wrappers and abstract folders.
- Use framework conventions first. Add project conventions only when they make ownership or boundaries clearer.

## App Layout

```
app/
├── api/                  # Route handlers
├── <route>/              # App Router segment
│   ├── page.tsx          # Public route entry
│   ├── layout.tsx        # Segment layout when needed
│   ├── loading.tsx       # Segment loading UI when needed
│   ├── error.tsx         # Segment error boundary when needed
│   ├── _components/      # Route-private UI
│   ├── _hooks/           # Route-private React hooks
│   ├── _lib/             # Route-private logic, helpers, constants
│   └── _types/           # Route-private types
├── components/           # Shared UI used by multiple routes
├── hooks/                # Shared hooks used by multiple routes
├── lib/                  # Shared utilities and integrations
│   └── server/           # Server-only code
└── types/                # Shared type declarations
```

Do not introduce `src/` unless the whole app is intentionally migrated. This repo uses root `app/`.

## Colocation

- Put route-owned code in that route's `_components`, `_hooks`, `_lib`, or `_types` folder.
- Move code to `app/components`, `app/hooks`, `app/lib`, or `app/types` only when it is used by multiple routes or is clearly route-independent.
- Use private folders (`_folder`) for implementation details inside `app/`.
- Use route groups (`(group)`) only to organize routes or layouts without changing URLs.
- Keep API route handlers thin: parse the request, call server logic, and return a response.

## Naming

- Use `kebab-case` for all filenames, including component files: `chat-message.tsx`, `theme-toggle.tsx`, `model-viewer-state.ts`.
- Keep Next.js special filenames exact: `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `route.ts`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`.
- Use `PascalCase` for exported React component names and TypeScript interfaces.
- Use `camelCase` for functions, variables, and hook names. Hook files should start with `use-`.
- Name files after the main thing they contain. Avoid vague names like `utils.ts`, `helpers.ts`, or `common.ts` unless the scope is very small.

## File Size And Extraction

- Aim for normal source files around 150-250 lines.
- Above 300 lines, look for a meaningful split: component, hook, parser, state machine, data mapper, or domain helper.
- Above 500 lines, split unless the file is generated, a data table, or a tightly cohesive complex module.
- Extract code when it has a clear responsibility, a useful name, or real reuse. Do not extract just to move five lines elsewhere.
- Keep React components focused on rendering and interaction. Move reusable stateful behavior to hooks and pure logic to `_lib`.

## Imports And Boundaries

- Use the `@/` path alias for imports from the project root.
- Keep imports at the top of the file. Use `next/dynamic` for intentional client-side code splitting.
- Do not create barrel files. Import directly from the source module.
- Do not re-export types through components or hooks. Import types from `_types` or `app/types`.
- Use `import type` for type-only imports.
- Code in `app/lib/server` is server-only and must not be imported by Client Components.

## React And Next.js

- Server Components are the default. Add `"use client"` only for browser APIs, state, effects, event handlers, or client-only libraries.
- Keep Client Components as small as practical and pass only the data they need.
- Fetch data on the server when possible. Use `Promise.all` for independent async work.
- Prefer deriving values during render over syncing state in effects.
- Use refs for transient values that should not trigger renders.
- Use functional state updates when the next state depends on the previous state.
- Memoize only when there is a measured or obvious render cost.

## TypeScript And Code Quality

- Strict mode is enabled. Do not weaken types to make errors disappear.
- Do not use `any`. If a cast is unavoidable, cast to a specific type.
- Use `import type` for type-only imports.
- Prefer interfaces for object shapes, type aliases for unions and intersections.
- Exhaustive switch handling: all discriminated union switches must handle every case or have a `never` default.
- Prefer early returns over nested conditionals.
- Use `Map` or `Set` for repeated ID or membership lookups.
- Avoid narrating comments. Comment non-obvious intent, constraints, or trade-offs.
- Keep edits focused. Do not rewrite unrelated code while touching a file.
